## JVM configuration for Logstash on t3.2xlarge (32 GB RAM).
##
## WHERE THIS FILE GOES:
##   scripts/logstash-deploy.sh copies config/logstash/config/* to /etc/logstash/
##   on the EC2 instance, so this file lands at /etc/logstash/jvm.options and
##   replaces the defaults bundled with the Logstash RPM at install time.
##   Changes here take effect on the next deploy + service restart.
##
## WHY IT EXISTS:
##   Logstash's default heap is 1 GB. Under sustained EN pipeline load on
##   t3.2xlarge (32 GB RAM), the default heap saturated at 94%, triggering
##   GC stall and dropping output to 0 events/sec. See issue #107.
##   Setting -Xms/-Xmx to 8 GB resolved the stall immediately.
##
## BUNDLED JDK:
##   Logstash 8.x ships with a bundled OpenJDK 21 — no separate Java install
##   is needed or used on this instance. This file does not set JAVA_HOME.
##   If the instance type or Logstash major version changes, revisit all values.
##
## HEAP SIZING RULE:
##   Do not exceed half of total system RAM. Logstash's off-heap usage
##   (network buffers, JVM metaspace, native code) can match or exceed the
##   heap at high throughput, so the other half must remain free for the OS.

-Xms8g
-Xmx8g

################################################################
## Expert settings — only change with a specific reason
################################################################

## G1GC is the default on JDK 21; no explicit flags needed. CMS was removed
## in Java 14, so the 11-13:-XX:+UseConcMarkSweepGC lines common in older
## jvm.options templates are not included here.

## Locale
-Dfile.encoding=UTF-8

## JRuby settings:
## invokedynamic: enables JVM method-handle optimizations for JRuby's dynamic
##   dispatch, improving throughput at the cost of a longer warmup period.
## jit.threshold=0: compile all JRuby methods to JVM bytecode immediately
##   (no interpreter warm-up), which reduces latency on cold filter execution.
-Djruby.compile.invokedynamic=true
-Djruby.jit.threshold=0

## Dump heap on OOM for post-mortem analysis
-XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError
